WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.960
Help all kids learn and grow with PBS Kids.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.240
Thank you for supporting your PBS station.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.600
Hi, it's the Krapp Brothers here.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:10.320
I'm Chris.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:11.620
And I'm Martin.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.730
When most animals jump in the water, they go under, right?

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.430
I mean, dogs go under, kids go under.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.230
But water striders, an incredible insect that walks on water,

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.350
effortlessly stand on the surface.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.440
How do they do it?

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.180
For one thing, they're very light.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.980
And they have teeny tiny...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.320
hairs on their legs that hold on to microscopic air bubbles that act like natural flotation

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:35.840
devices.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.300
That gives this animal the power to walk on water.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.840
Oh, I want to walk on water.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:41.520
Me too.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.650
Hey, are you thinking what I'm thinking, bro?

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:44.550
Oh yeah.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:45.570
Let's go!

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.630
Skiing is one way to walk on water.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.540
And it does get you going on the surface, but not 100% natural.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.230
For one thing, you need a lot of speed.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.870
and a 350 whor-

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.300
horsepower engine to get you going.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:03.560
Yahoo!

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:10.010
We're walking on water.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.670
Believe it or not, there is another creature much bigger than the water strider that can

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:29.250
walk on the water with 100% of its own creature power.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:29.970
The-

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.280
The Basilisk.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:37.620
This creature has the ability to walk and run across the surface of the water.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.520
Imagine if we had the Basilisk's creature powers.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:41.730
What if?

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:18.250
is it my fucking

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:35.370
Walk on the wet side.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:40.500
Sign of them?

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:41.400
Not yet.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:42.970
What were they looking for again?

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.440
I'm not sure, but I think it was-

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.520
Wait, there they are.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:47.300
Woo-hoo!

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.330
That's them all right.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.640
Let's put the tortuga down in that clearing.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.230
Yeah, the ultimate water-walking lizard, the basilisk.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.440
A basilisk, so that's it.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.700
What a beautiful lizard.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.530
Looks like a dinosaur to me, and it walks on water?

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:29.970
Yeah, you heard that?

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.800
The basilisks can actually run on water.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.390
It's amazing! How's it doing?

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.970
To pull off water walking, you have to be fast!

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.830
But basilisks are fast with their own power.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.470
We're being pulled by a boat!

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.360
How do they do it?

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.620
Oh, we've got to find one and find out!

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.730
And find out fast!

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:53.810
I'm on it!

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.460
Attention, Wildcraft kids in Costa Rica!

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.980
If you see any water walking basilisk lizards...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.440
Let us know. Whoa, that was fast.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:08.910
Hey guys, it's Nina. I love basilisk lizards.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.110
Sometimes I can even see them from my house.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:12.920
They like to sit in the sun by the river.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:14.470
I'm sending my location.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.140
That's great, Nina. Nina? Huh, that's weird.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.520
The transmission cut out.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.790
Don't worry, I was able to pinpoint a location.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:28.940
Hey, gimme!

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.890
We need to stay on the surface.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.180
But Cokie just found out a wild crack kid knows where to find those water walking lizards.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.320
Really? Where?

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:45.260
It's up river somewhere. Cokie's sending the info to your creature pods.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.810
Oh yeah! Water walking basilisks, here we come!

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:56.230
I know you guys know a lot about creepy creatures, but lizards that walk on water?

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.220
Yeah, sure. And I always walk on water.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:04:59.800
When?

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:00.680
And it rains!

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:01.980
Nice!

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:09.530
Canoe stays on the water's surface because it's buoyant,

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.970
meaning the weight is spread out over a wide area, allowing it to float.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:18.590
So how does a basilisk lizard walk on water without anything big and buoyant to keep it afloat?

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:20.640
That's what we're about to figure out.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:25.650
And one of the best ways to find them is by canoe,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.980
because they like to hang out on jobo trees that hang out over the...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.810
And as soon as you do, I'll program a basilisk disc

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.310
that gives your suits the power to walk on water.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:38.840
Cool! See ya, Viva!

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.960
Hey, Koki, still haven't received the coordinates

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.580
of where Nina saw the basilisk.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:47.480
Working on it.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.580
My communication terminal is cutting in and out.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:52.620
Okay, we'll start heading upriver.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.340
Send it when you get it.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.240
Terminal's heading out.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.280
Maybe the Krat boys are running around with something that would make an exotic hat.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.380
Have you intercepted their terminal yet?

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:07.630
Maybe?

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:09.000
Maybe!

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.200
Won't win me the best designer hat prize in Paris, Dobby-o!

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:17.530
I need a truly inspirational creature to make the most beautiful hat in the world.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.790
And those wild crafts are going to help me find it.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:20.790
Now shove over!

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.260
Okay, we've got the location, Koki.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.680
We'll let you know when we find the waterwalking Basilisk Lizard.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.940
A water-walking lizard hat can win with that!

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:38.280
Turn the jet around, Davio. We're going to Costa Rica.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.830
Right. This is the place Nina said she saw the basilisk lizard.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.530
Oh, yeah. Jobo trees.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:52.720
A basilisk loves to lay in the sun in the morning on a jobo tree and eat the yummy fruit.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:56.980
